WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:01.940
- Legal Eagle is coming in loud and clear

00:00:01.940 --> 00:00:04.220
thanks to Ting Mobile.

00:00:04.220 --> 00:00:07.170
Before President Trump's
photo op at St. John's Church

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:08.420
in front of Lafayette Square,

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:10.020
which resulted in peaceful protestors

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:11.080
being gassed with chemicals

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:12.340
and shot with rubber bullets,

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:14.950
he appeared in the Rose
Garden behind the White House

00:00:14.950 --> 00:00:17.200
to outline his response to the protests

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:18.940
that have roiled this nation.

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:21.450
President Trump threatened
to take dramatic action

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:22.840
to quell the protests,

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:25.940
including invoking the
Insurrection Act of 1807,

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:28.530
which allegedly would allow
him to deploy the military

00:00:28.530 --> 00:00:31.100
to crack down on protesters and rioters.

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:32.580
President Trump did not officially

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:33.740
invoke the Insurrection Act,

00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:36.220
but he did use his unique
authority within the district

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:38.330
to make a serious show of force.

00:00:38.330 --> 00:00:39.163
In recent days,

00:00:39.163 --> 00:00:41.100
for reasons that should
be relatively obvious,

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:42.770
people have debated how much authority

00:00:42.770 --> 00:00:44.590
the president has to use the military

00:00:44.590 --> 00:00:46.740
for civilian law enforcement purposes.

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:48.370
And in addition to the Insurrection Act,

00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:50.774
you probably heard people
mention the Posse Comitatus Act

00:00:50.774 --> 00:00:52.400
and claim that the president actually

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:54.610
doesn't have authority to
invoke the Armed Forces

00:00:54.610 --> 00:00:55.890
in riots and protests.

00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:58.330
Well, I'm here to
deliver the sobering news

00:00:58.330 --> 00:01:00.350
the president actually
has a lot of authority

00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:03.240
to use the military against
the American people.

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:04.590
But whether he can do it

00:01:04.590 --> 00:01:06.070
and whether he should do it

00:01:06.070 --> 00:01:07.855
are two completely separate issues.

00:01:07.855 --> 00:01:10.272
(epic music)

00:01:12.310 --> 00:01:13.143
Hey, Legal Eagles.

00:01:13.143 --> 00:01:14.390
It's time to think like a lawyer

00:01:14.390 --> 00:01:16.910
because I can personally
confirm that there are

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:18.412
a lot of American troops

00:01:18.412 --> 00:01:21.600
operating on American soil as we speak.

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:22.433
And by the way,

00:01:22.433 --> 00:01:24.040
I started my research for this video

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:25.920
by going to what I think is by far

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:28.040
the best resource for
national security law.

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:29.710
And that's a blog called Lawfare.

00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:31.220
It's simply, bar none,

00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:33.710
the best resource for answers

00:01:33.710 --> 00:01:35.570
to national security law questions.

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:37.700
You can go there at lawfareblog.com,

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:39.060
I highly recommend it.

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:41.330
So let's start with the
Posse Comitatus Act.

00:01:41.330 --> 00:01:42.710
Does it stop the president

00:01:42.710 --> 00:01:44.670
from using the military domestically?

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:47.530
Now it's easy to confuse
the Insurrection Act of 1807

00:01:47.530 --> 00:01:49.160
with the Posse Comitatus Act.

00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:50.920
In fact, you'll often see people say that

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:52.980
the PCA actually bars the president

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:55.490
from using the military
in domestic matters.

00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:57.500
And there's some truth to it,

00:01:57.500 --> 00:01:58.910
but it's largely false.

00:01:58.910 --> 00:02:02.797
Section 1385 of Title 18
of the US Code states,

00:02:02.797 --> 00:02:04.427
"Whoever, except in cases

00:02:04.427 --> 00:02:06.487
"and under circumstances
expressly authorized

00:02:06.487 --> 00:02:08.617
"by the Constitution or Act of Congress,

00:02:08.617 --> 00:02:11.127
"willfully uses any part of
the Army or the Air Force

00:02:11.127 --> 00:02:14.687
"as a posse comitatus or
otherwise to execute the laws

00:02:14.687 --> 00:02:16.357
"shall be fined under this title

00:02:16.357 --> 00:02:19.370
"or imprisoned not more
than two years, or both."

00:02:19.370 --> 00:02:21.470
Now a big part of a
lawyer's job is analyzing

00:02:21.470 --> 00:02:23.530
the text of laws and judicial opinions.

00:02:23.530 --> 00:02:26.100
And you'll notice that this
language provides a bar

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:29.290
unless there is a specific act of Congress

00:02:29.290 --> 00:02:31.800
or something in the
Constitution that allows it.

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:33.140
This is one of those situations where

00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:35.360
the exception predominates the rule.

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:37.100
And generally, the Posse Comitatus Act

00:02:37.100 --> 00:02:39.330
prevents the military from participating

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:41.840
in traditional law enforcement activities.

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:43.700
However, Congress has enacted many,

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:45.260
many exceptions to the PCA

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:47.440
to allow the military to assist civilian

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:50.080
law enforcement agencies
in enforcing the laws.

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:51.660
For example, the Department of Defense

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:54.340
can provide some support
to law enforcement agencies

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:56.450
battling drug crimes and organzine crime.

00:02:56.450 --> 00:02:59.200
The military may also defend
their own bases and property.

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:00.820
It's one of those instances where

00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:03.320
the exceptions tend to swallow the rule.

00:03:03.320 --> 00:03:05.460
It's kinda like the rule against hearsay.

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:07.320
Sure, there's a general rule against

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:08.950
hearsay being admissible in court.

00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:11.073
But, frankly, there are so
many different exceptions

00:03:11.073 --> 00:03:13.200
that it's actually the rarity

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:14.800
where you can't get the hearsay

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:16.840
admitted into court as evidence.

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:18.920
But the biggest exception to the PCA

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:21.260
is the Insurrection Act itself,

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:22.800
so let's talk about that.

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:24.620
The Insurrection Act of 1807

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:26.770
actually refers to five
pieces of legislation

00:03:26.770 --> 00:03:30.550
that are found in 10 USC,
Sections 251 through 255.

00:03:30.550 --> 00:03:32.360
The law sets forth several scenarios

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:33.470
that a president may use

00:03:33.470 --> 00:03:36.150
to justify deploying the
military domestically.

00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:37.810
Now, before the president can invoke

00:03:37.810 --> 00:03:39.517
the Insurrection Act, he quote,

00:03:39.517 --> 00:03:41.427
"Must first issue a proclamation

00:03:41.427 --> 00:03:45.190
"ordering the insurgents to
disperse within a limited time."

00:03:45.190 --> 00:03:47.220
If the situation does not resolve itself,

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:49.300
the the president may
issue an executive order

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:50.553
to send in troops.

00:03:50.553 --> 00:03:53.010
How, it's important to note
that the Insurrection Act

00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:54.580
actually dates back to a time when

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:55.810
municipal Police Departments

00:03:55.810 --> 00:03:57.890
didn't really exist in this country.

00:03:57.890 --> 00:03:59.886
And so, it made sense
that there would be times

00:03:59.886 --> 00:04:01.780
when the federal government would need

00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:04.140
to send military personnel to help out

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:06.100
with state or local issues.

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:08.340
Now, given that in the modern era,

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:12.740
ever state and every municipality
has a Police Department,

00:04:12.740 --> 00:04:15.000
one might question the utility of having

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:17.510
the Insurrection Act
remaining on the books.

00:04:17.510 --> 00:04:20.220
Now, the oldest section of
this law is Section 254,

00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:22.670
which was enacted in 1792

00:04:22.670 --> 00:04:24.180
to give George Washington authority

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:25.510
to call forth the militia

00:04:25.510 --> 00:04:26.870
to repel invasions,

00:04:26.870 --> 00:04:27.970
suppress insurrections,

00:04:27.970 --> 00:04:29.540
or enforce the laws.

00:04:29.540 --> 00:04:31.030
Congress was initially reluctant

00:04:31.030 --> 00:04:33.280
to grant even Washington this authority,

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:34.820
so they required that he would first need

00:04:34.820 --> 00:04:36.710
to give insurgents a warning to disperse.

00:04:36.710 --> 00:04:38.740
And although President Trump
has explicit threatened

00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:40.410
to invoke the Insurrection Act.

00:04:40.410 --> 00:04:43.410
- If a city or state
refuses to take the actions

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:46.566
that are necessary to
defend the life and property

00:04:46.566 --> 00:04:48.310
of their residents,

00:04:48.310 --> 00:04:51.164
then I will deploy the
United States military

00:04:51.164 --> 00:04:55.120
and quickly solve the problem for them.

00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:58.220
- To date, it's not clear
that his speech qualifies

00:04:58.220 --> 00:05:00.770
as a proclamation required under this act.

00:05:00.770 --> 00:05:02.850
Section 251 of the Insurrection Act

00:05:02.850 --> 00:05:05.100
deals with federal aid
for state governments.

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:08.240
It combines two laws from the
earliest days of the Republic.

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:10.020
One was a law used to suppress

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:12.320
the Whiskey Rebellion of 1794.

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:14.540
This was an uprising of
farmers and distillers

00:05:14.540 --> 00:05:17.060
who protested a federal whiskey tax.

00:05:17.060 --> 00:05:19.590
After years of protesters
tarring and feathering

00:05:19.590 --> 00:05:21.440
tax collectors and destroying property,

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:23.080
President Washington was given authority

00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:25.340
to call forth the militia
upon the request of a governor

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:26.640
or state legislature.

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:29.100
The second part of this
law was enacted in 1807

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:30.730
at the request of President Jefferson,

00:05:30.730 --> 00:05:32.960
and allows the president
to call up a standing army

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:34.740
as well as the militia.

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:36.550
Which brings us to Section 252,

00:05:36.550 --> 00:05:38.780
which regards to the use
of militia and Armed Forces

00:05:38.780 --> 00:05:40.760
to enforce federal authority.

00:05:40.760 --> 00:05:43.390
Section 252 was enacted in 1861

00:05:43.390 --> 00:05:45.000
at the request of President Lincoln,

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:46.570
to increase presidential authority

00:05:46.570 --> 00:05:48.420
to use the militia and regular army

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:51.280
to suppress insurrections
and enforce the laws.

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:54.070
This law was the legal basis
for waging the Civil War.

00:05:54.070 --> 00:05:55.700
And the law allows the president

00:05:55.700 --> 00:05:58.040
to use federal troops
on his own initiative

00:05:58.040 --> 00:05:59.690
and act on his own judgment

00:05:59.690 --> 00:06:01.820
without having to wait for
the request of a governor.

00:06:01.820 --> 00:06:04.700
And, in fact, you're seeing
a lot of governors on TV

00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:06.530
recently stating that they are not

00:06:06.530 --> 00:06:09.080
going to request federal
troops or federal aid

00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:10.240
under the Insurrection Act.

00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:12.840
The thing is, for most
of the Insurrection Act,

00:06:12.840 --> 00:06:16.270
it doesn't require gubernatorial approval.

00:06:16.270 --> 00:06:18.020
That brings us to Section 253,

00:06:18.020 --> 00:06:20.120
Interference with State and Federal Law.

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:23.440
In 1869, Congress added section 253

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:25.160
at the request of President Grant

00:06:25.160 --> 00:06:26.930
to empower him to use federal troops

00:06:26.930 --> 00:06:29.920
to suppress the Ku Klux
Klan during Reconstruction.

00:06:29.920 --> 00:06:31.507
Section 253 states, quote,

00:06:31.507 --> 00:06:33.957
"The president, by using the
militia or the Armed Forces,

00:06:33.957 --> 00:06:35.987
"or both, or by any other means,

00:06:35.987 --> 00:06:38.437
"shall take such measures
as he considers necessary

00:06:38.437 --> 00:06:40.637
"to suppress in a State any insurrection,

00:06:40.637 --> 00:06:43.327
"domestic violence, unlawful
combination or conspiracy,

00:06:43.327 --> 00:06:45.547
"if it, one, so hinders the execution

00:06:45.547 --> 00:06:46.497
"of the laws of that State

00:06:46.497 --> 00:06:48.297
"and of the United
States within the State,

00:06:48.297 --> 00:06:50.797
"that any part or class of
its people is deprived of

00:06:50.797 --> 00:06:52.137
"a right, privilege, immunity,

00:06:52.137 --> 00:06:54.177
"or protection named in the Constitution,

00:06:54.177 --> 00:06:55.627
"and secured by law,

00:06:55.627 --> 00:06:57.397
"and the constituted
authorities of that State

00:06:57.397 --> 00:06:59.677
"are unable, fail or refuse to protect

00:06:59.677 --> 00:07:01.357
"that right, privilege or immunity,

00:07:01.357 --> 00:07:02.757
"or to give that protection;

00:07:02.757 --> 00:07:05.577
"or, two, opposes or
obstructs the execution

00:07:05.577 --> 00:07:06.907
"of the laws of the United States,

00:07:06.907 --> 00:07:09.940
"or impedes the course of
justice under those laws."

00:07:09.940 --> 00:07:13.360
And you can tell by the plain
text of this language that

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:15.550
that is incredibly broad authority.

00:07:15.550 --> 00:07:18.820
And that in a situation where
you have peaceful protesters,

00:07:18.820 --> 00:07:20.540
and rioting and looting going on

00:07:20.540 --> 00:07:22.070
at the exact same time,

00:07:22.070 --> 00:07:25.190
that it's not surprising
that someone might use

00:07:25.190 --> 00:07:27.700
the unlawful activity as a pretext

00:07:27.700 --> 00:07:31.000
to curtail the lawful activity as well.

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:33.830
Now, originally there were
two fairly large limits

00:07:33.830 --> 00:07:35.620
on the use of military personnel

00:07:35.620 --> 00:07:38.050
by invoking the Insurrection
Act by the president.

00:07:38.050 --> 00:07:40.670
The first was that the
president had to get approval

00:07:40.670 --> 00:07:42.130
from a federal judge.

00:07:42.130 --> 00:07:43.950
The second was that the Insurrection Act

00:07:43.950 --> 00:07:46.910
could be used until Congress
came back into session,

00:07:46.910 --> 00:07:49.840
and then had to approve
the use of the military.

00:07:49.840 --> 00:07:52.420
Now, those two restrictions
on the Insurrection Act

00:07:52.420 --> 00:07:55.640
sunsetted out and no
longer are part of the act.

00:07:55.640 --> 00:07:57.370
And no longer act as a check

00:07:57.370 --> 00:07:59.389
against the use of military personnel

00:07:59.389 --> 00:08:01.230
under the Insurrection Act.

00:08:01.230 --> 00:08:03.940
So when has the Insurrection Act exception

00:08:03.940 --> 00:08:06.120
been used to Posse Comitatus?

00:08:06.120 --> 00:08:07.500
Now, although the Insurrection Act

00:08:07.500 --> 00:08:08.880
has not been used frequently,

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:11.190
presidents have invoked
it to enforce the law,

00:08:11.190 --> 00:08:13.620
especially when racial
equality is at stake.

00:08:13.620 --> 00:08:16.130
In 1957, President Eisenhower called on

00:08:16.130 --> 00:08:18.560
the US Army's 101st Airborne Division

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:20.630
to safely escort nine black students

00:08:20.630 --> 00:08:22.640
into Little Rock Central High School

00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:25.290
after the Arkansas governor, Orval Faubus,

00:08:25.290 --> 00:08:27.220
had called on the Arkansas National Guard

00:08:27.220 --> 00:08:29.680
to stop the students from
integrating the school.

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:33.290
President Kennedy invoked
the act in 1962 and 1963

00:08:33.290 --> 00:08:35.470
to send federal troops to
Mississippi and Alabama

00:08:35.470 --> 00:08:37.250
to enforce civil rights laws.

00:08:37.250 --> 00:08:39.580
In 1967, President Johnson sent

00:08:39.580 --> 00:08:43.010
the 82nd and 101st Airborne
Divisions to Detroit

00:08:43.010 --> 00:08:44.890
to quell riots between
police and residents

00:08:44.890 --> 00:08:47.270
after Martin Luther King
Jr. was assassinated.

00:08:47.270 --> 00:08:49.570
And in 1992, President H. W. Bush

00:08:49.570 --> 00:08:50.900
responded to a request from

00:08:50.900 --> 00:08:52.800
Governor Pete Wilson of California

00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:54.640
to quell rioting in Los Angeles

00:08:54.640 --> 00:08:56.870
after the acquittal of
the four LAPD officers

00:08:56.870 --> 00:08:58.560
who brutally beat Rodney King.

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:01.600
- There are 3,000
national guardsmen on duty

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:03.120
in the city of Los Angeles.

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:04.450
And let me assure you,

00:09:04.450 --> 00:09:09.180
I will use whatever force is
necessary to restore order.

00:09:09.180 --> 00:09:10.210
- So in modern times,

00:09:10.210 --> 00:09:12.640
the presidential use
of the Insurrection Act

00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:14.880
has generally been used to enforce

00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:17.730
civil rights, and integration,
and racial equality

00:09:17.730 --> 00:09:18.870
rather than the opposite.

00:09:18.870 --> 00:09:21.450
And it's generally been used in ways

00:09:21.450 --> 00:09:24.080
that most people would
find unobjectionable,

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:26.280
at least in this day and age.

00:09:26.280 --> 00:09:28.680
And sometimes that use
of the Insurrection Act

00:09:28.680 --> 00:09:32.340
was explicitly against the
wishes of the state governors.

00:09:32.340 --> 00:09:33.760
For example, Governor Faubus

00:09:33.760 --> 00:09:35.840
had improperly used the
State National Guard

00:09:35.840 --> 00:09:38.420
to disregard federal court orders,

00:09:38.420 --> 00:09:40.850
regarding integration of students.

00:09:40.850 --> 00:09:42.100
And in that example,

00:09:42.100 --> 00:09:43.650
he certainly did not want

00:09:43.650 --> 00:09:45.720
the federal government's assistance.

00:09:45.720 --> 00:09:48.920
In fact, that ran completely
contrary to what he wanted.

00:09:48.920 --> 00:09:50.260
Governor Wilson, on the other hand,

00:09:50.260 --> 00:09:52.550
did ask for help from President Bush.

00:09:52.550 --> 00:09:54.630
And the other instances also involved

00:09:54.630 --> 00:09:56.840
quelling of riots that
were explicit responses

00:09:56.840 --> 00:09:58.730
to racial injustice.

00:09:58.730 --> 00:10:00.880
And there's probably one
other historical example

00:10:00.880 --> 00:10:02.240
that is incredibly relevant.

00:10:02.240 --> 00:10:04.080
And that was the federal
government's response

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:05.700
to Hurricane Katrina.

00:10:05.700 --> 00:10:07.670
When the levees in Louisiana broke,

00:10:07.670 --> 00:10:09.760
the state and federal
government were slow to respond

00:10:09.760 --> 00:10:11.500
to the enormous logistical challenges

00:10:11.500 --> 00:10:13.200
and humanitarian crisis.

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:15.870
President Bush considered
invoking the Insurrection Act,

00:10:15.870 --> 00:10:17.970
but the Department of
Defense was concerned

00:10:17.970 --> 00:10:19.400
that Americans would be reluctant

00:10:19.400 --> 00:10:21.080
to see the military in that role.

00:10:21.080 --> 00:10:24.500
And at the time the military
was fighting two wars,

00:10:24.500 --> 00:10:26.910
and eventually Congress
amended the Insurrection Act

00:10:26.910 --> 00:10:29.150
to clarify the circumstances
that would justify

00:10:29.150 --> 00:10:30.700
the use of Armed Forces.

00:10:30.700 --> 00:10:32.150
There is now language in the statute

00:10:32.150 --> 00:10:33.840
that lets the president use the military

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:36.219
to restore order and
enforce the federal laws

00:10:36.219 --> 00:10:39.937
if there is a natural disaster,
epidemic, or terrorist act.

00:10:39.937 --> 00:10:42.110
Though, ultimately,
the Bush administration

00:10:42.110 --> 00:10:44.370
decided not to invoke the Insurrection Act

00:10:44.370 --> 00:10:48.790
in support of humanitarian
efforts regarding Katrina.

00:10:48.790 --> 00:10:50.640
And it's probably important to talk

00:10:50.640 --> 00:10:54.010
about the distinction
between norms and laws.

00:10:54.010 --> 00:10:58.030
The law itself has been pretty
broad for a very long time,

00:10:58.030 --> 00:11:00.360
but it's revealing that
presidents have been reluctant

00:11:00.360 --> 00:11:02.160
to invoke the Insurrection Act.

00:11:02.160 --> 00:11:05.580
That is a presidential
norm, a societal norm,

00:11:05.580 --> 00:11:07.850
but it is not written into the Act.

00:11:07.850 --> 00:11:10.690
And you can see how a broadly written law

00:11:10.690 --> 00:11:13.250
that has been used in some instances

00:11:13.250 --> 00:11:14.710
where no one would object,

00:11:14.710 --> 00:11:15.960
and probably other instances

00:11:15.960 --> 00:11:17.750
that were closer to the margins,

00:11:17.750 --> 00:11:19.880
can be used for good and ill

00:11:19.880 --> 00:11:24.100
depending on who is upholding
the norms and who is not.

00:11:24.100 --> 00:11:26.010
And the Katrina example is important

00:11:26.010 --> 00:11:27.740
because it reveals two concerns

00:11:27.740 --> 00:11:29.540
that normally come up
when there's a situation

00:11:29.540 --> 00:11:31.350
requiring a national response.

00:11:31.350 --> 00:11:33.240
And that also involves the difference

00:11:33.240 --> 00:11:35.000
between norms and laws.

00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:36.870
First, the American people
have traditionally been

00:11:36.870 --> 00:11:39.970
very wary about military
operating on US soil.

00:11:39.970 --> 00:11:41.860
This, in turn, has made the military wary

00:11:41.860 --> 00:11:43.840
of being deployed for civilian matters.

00:11:43.840 --> 00:11:46.180
And there are very good policy reasons

00:11:46.180 --> 00:11:47.740
that should probably be pretty obvious

00:11:47.740 --> 00:11:50.630
as to why you don't want a military

00:11:50.630 --> 00:11:52.340
doing law enforcement activity

00:11:52.340 --> 00:11:54.530
and why you want a strong separation

00:11:54.530 --> 00:11:56.178
between a military that's trained to kill,

00:11:56.178 --> 00:11:59.380
and a police force that is
trained to serve and protect.

00:11:59.380 --> 00:12:02.470
And second, the Bush administration
had a significant debate

00:12:02.470 --> 00:12:03.940
about whether to invoke the act.

00:12:03.940 --> 00:12:07.450
Members of Congress weighed in
and so did all 50 governors.

00:12:07.450 --> 00:12:08.867
Under normal administrations,

00:12:08.867 --> 00:12:10.870
both Democratic and Republican,

00:12:10.870 --> 00:12:12.310
there's a decision-making process

00:12:12.310 --> 00:12:14.000
where the president
hears from stakeholders

00:12:14.000 --> 00:12:15.930
about the pros and cons
of using the military.

00:12:15.930 --> 00:12:17.600
That doesn't mean the
president lacks the power

00:12:17.600 --> 00:12:19.870
to use the military or enforce the law.

00:12:19.870 --> 00:12:23.080
On the contrary, the president
clearly has the discretion

00:12:23.080 --> 00:12:25.610
to involve the military
in civilian affairs

00:12:25.610 --> 00:12:27.250
under these different acts,

00:12:27.250 --> 00:12:29.440
even if the governors don't ask for help.

00:12:29.440 --> 00:12:30.730
but rather it signals that,

00:12:30.730 --> 00:12:31.690
at least in modern times,

00:12:31.690 --> 00:12:33.040
we've had presidents who thought

00:12:33.040 --> 00:12:34.680
about what the governor's might want;

00:12:34.680 --> 00:12:36.160
how the people might react;

00:12:36.160 --> 00:12:38.090
and whether a military response

00:12:38.090 --> 00:12:40.360
would actually make things worse.

00:12:40.360 --> 00:12:44.700
And it's hard not to think
about the historical example

00:12:44.700 --> 00:12:46.480
of Caesar crossing the Rubicon,

00:12:46.480 --> 00:12:51.480
that sometimes the norms involving
the lack of participation

00:12:52.040 --> 00:12:53.900
by the military and domestic affairs

00:12:53.900 --> 00:12:56.600
or at least crossing
the Rubicon into Rome,

00:12:56.600 --> 00:12:59.960
that that norm exists for
a very, very good reason.

00:12:59.960 --> 00:13:01.820
And it's also probably worth pointing out

00:13:01.820 --> 00:13:03.550
that the Posse Comitatus Act,

00:13:03.550 --> 00:13:05.260
in conjunction with the Insurrection Act,

00:13:05.260 --> 00:13:06.730
contemplate that the president is going

00:13:06.730 --> 00:13:08.870
to faithfully execute the laws.

00:13:08.870 --> 00:13:10.540
And that, on that basis,

00:13:10.540 --> 00:13:12.550
the president probably
has a lot of discretion

00:13:12.550 --> 00:13:14.696
to use the military to stop riots

00:13:14.696 --> 00:13:17.920
in the name of enforcing the laws,

00:13:17.920 --> 00:13:20.590
because we have a lot of laws out there.

00:13:20.590 --> 00:13:23.580
And it's hard to not think about this fact

00:13:23.580 --> 00:13:26.980
without examining the context
of what actually happened

00:13:26.980 --> 00:13:30.800
when the president mentioned
invoking the Insurrection Act.

00:13:30.800 --> 00:13:33.940
That while he was giving his
speech in the Rose Garden,

00:13:33.940 --> 00:13:36.510
that he or Bill Barr gave orders

00:13:36.510 --> 00:13:39.550
to clear out the protesters
in Lafayette Square

00:13:39.550 --> 00:13:41.160
so that the president
could get his photo op

00:13:41.160 --> 00:13:43.230
at Saint John's Church.

00:13:43.230 --> 00:13:45.924
To clear a path for the
president's impromptu walk,

00:13:45.924 --> 00:13:48.530
the US Federal Park Police used

00:13:48.530 --> 00:13:52.050
OC gas, pepper sprays, rubber
bullets, and flashbangs

00:13:52.050 --> 00:13:53.930
on a crowd of peaceful protesters.

00:13:53.930 --> 00:13:56.240
All of this happened
before the 7 p.m. curfew.

00:13:56.240 --> 00:13:58.040
And, of course, the right to protest,

00:13:58.040 --> 00:13:59.160
to peaceably assemble,

00:13:59.160 --> 00:14:00.510
is part of the First Amendment.

00:14:00.510 --> 00:14:02.160
This is part of the freedom of expression

00:14:02.160 --> 00:14:03.660
that all Americans enjoy.

00:14:03.660 --> 00:14:06.200
Protests are a way of
life in Washington, DC.

00:14:06.200 --> 00:14:07.100
Which is to be expected,

00:14:07.100 --> 00:14:08.840
this is after all the seat of government.

00:14:08.840 --> 00:14:11.300
But it doesn't mean that
President Trump's actions

00:14:11.300 --> 00:14:14.800
on Monday were necessarily
illegal in the strictest sense.

00:14:14.800 --> 00:14:15.880
Over the last several decades,

00:14:15.880 --> 00:14:18.160
courts have allowed government
to make more restrictions

00:14:18.160 --> 00:14:19.970
on the time, place,
and manner of protests.

00:14:19.970 --> 00:14:21.492
As long as those restrictions are

00:14:21.492 --> 00:14:23.780
what's called content neutral.

00:14:23.780 --> 00:14:25.840
And you can imagine how some of these laws

00:14:25.840 --> 00:14:28.350
conflict with individual citizens' right

00:14:28.350 --> 00:14:30.170
to First Amendment activity,

00:14:30.170 --> 00:14:31.400
to petition the government,

00:14:31.400 --> 00:14:34.320
to peaceably assemble and to protest.

00:14:34.320 --> 00:14:36.060
There are, in fact, many statutes that

00:14:36.060 --> 00:14:37.520
make it clear that protesters cannot

00:14:37.520 --> 00:14:39.490
block access to federal buildings,

00:14:39.490 --> 00:14:41.020
or destroy private property,

00:14:41.020 --> 00:14:44.170
or stop the president from
going somewhere he wants to go.

00:14:44.170 --> 00:14:46.200
But you can also imagine
how some of these laws

00:14:46.200 --> 00:14:48.944
might be used as a pretext

00:14:48.944 --> 00:14:52.000
to quell First Amendment
expressive activity.

00:14:52.000 --> 00:14:53.340
And one of the things that made

00:14:53.340 --> 00:14:55.890
President Trump's actions
so incendiary is that

00:14:55.890 --> 00:14:57.300
he wasn't on his way to the Capitol

00:14:57.300 --> 00:14:58.230
to meet with Congress,

00:14:58.230 --> 00:15:00.050
nor was he doing anything strictly related

00:15:00.050 --> 00:15:01.470
to the business of being president.

00:15:01.470 --> 00:15:04.507
Instead, he just had an impromptu photo op

00:15:04.507 --> 00:15:08.330
that appears to be used for
his reelection campaign,

00:15:08.330 --> 00:15:10.080
that wasn't planned out,

00:15:10.080 --> 00:15:13.320
and specifically conflicted
with the protesters

00:15:13.320 --> 00:15:16.660
who were already there and
were already protesting

00:15:16.660 --> 00:15:19.498
peaceably using their
First Amendment rights.

00:15:19.498 --> 00:15:22.450
But that's the difference between norms

00:15:22.450 --> 00:15:25.020
and things that are written into the law

00:15:25.020 --> 00:15:29.220
And perhaps we've just been
lucky that, prior to now,

00:15:29.220 --> 00:15:31.500
we haven't had such a bumbling example

00:15:31.500 --> 00:15:35.270
of a pretext used to evacuate people

00:15:35.270 --> 00:15:37.410
exercising their First Amendment rights

00:15:37.410 --> 00:15:38.610
in front of the White House.

00:15:38.610 --> 00:15:41.260
And the same goes for the use of troops,

00:15:41.260 --> 00:15:43.270
and military and federal officers

00:15:43.270 --> 00:15:46.020
throughout DC and the rest of the country.

00:15:46.020 --> 00:15:47.970
Even President Nixon never placed soldiers

00:15:47.970 --> 00:15:49.210
at the foot of the Lincoln Memorial,

00:15:49.210 --> 00:15:51.290
blocking the American
people from accessing

00:15:51.290 --> 00:15:53.570
one of its most significant memorials,

00:15:53.570 --> 00:15:56.800
which we have seen that the
Trump administration has done.

00:15:56.800 --> 00:16:00.230
And it's been a long time
since all of Lafayette Square

00:16:00.230 --> 00:16:03.610
and such a significant portion
of downtown Washington, DC

00:16:03.610 --> 00:16:07.020
has been completely locked
off from protesters,

00:16:07.020 --> 00:16:08.190
or even tourists.

00:16:08.190 --> 00:16:12.020
I participated in the
Women's March in 2017.

00:16:12.020 --> 00:16:13.670
There were tens of thousands,

00:16:13.670 --> 00:16:15.640
if not hundreds of thousands of people,

00:16:15.640 --> 00:16:18.640
who were allowed to walk right
in front of the White House.

00:16:18.640 --> 00:16:20.800
And even that wouldn't be available

00:16:20.800 --> 00:16:22.450
based on what's going on right now.

00:16:22.450 --> 00:16:25.320
And, in fact, in a direct
historical parallel,

00:16:25.320 --> 00:16:27.080
when mass protests erupted

00:16:27.080 --> 00:16:29.550
after students were killed
by the National Guard

00:16:29.550 --> 00:16:31.100
at Kent State University,

00:16:31.100 --> 00:16:33.280
then President Nixon left the White House

00:16:33.280 --> 00:16:36.510
and met with protesters personally
at the Lincoln Memorial.

00:16:36.510 --> 00:16:38.870
This surreal event eventually led to Nixon

00:16:38.870 --> 00:16:40.780
creating a formal study of campus unrest

00:16:40.780 --> 00:16:41.930
during the Vietnam War.

00:16:41.930 --> 00:16:45.180
But because of Washington,
DC's unique position

00:16:45.180 --> 00:16:47.720
as a federal district,
rather than a state,

00:16:47.720 --> 00:16:49.660
and because of its disenfranchisement,

00:16:49.660 --> 00:16:51.520
the federal government has authority

00:16:51.520 --> 00:16:55.710
to use federal troops and
military police officers

00:16:55.710 --> 00:16:58.240
in the limits of Washington, DC

00:16:58.240 --> 00:17:00.530
in a way that they actually can't do

00:17:00.530 --> 00:17:02.130
outside of the district.

00:17:02.130 --> 00:17:03.630
So if it sounds like President Trump

00:17:03.630 --> 00:17:06.140
has a lot of power, he does.

00:17:06.140 --> 00:17:08.420
Presidents, in general,
have a lot of power

00:17:08.420 --> 00:17:10.990
to use the military for different ends.

00:17:10.990 --> 00:17:12.940
But as things stand, it's up in the air.

00:17:12.940 --> 00:17:14.060
It's not clear that the president

00:17:14.060 --> 00:17:16.380
has actually invoked the Insurrection Act.

00:17:16.380 --> 00:17:18.030
Secretary of Defense, Mike Esper,

00:17:18.030 --> 00:17:19.170
has voiced his opinion that

00:17:19.170 --> 00:17:20.600
the president should not, in fact,

00:17:20.600 --> 00:17:22.040
invoke the Insurrection Act,

00:17:22.040 --> 00:17:24.680
and should refrain from
using the US military

00:17:24.680 --> 00:17:28.600
to conduct law enforcement
and anti-riot actions.

00:17:28.600 --> 00:17:30.370
- The option to use active duty forces

00:17:30.370 --> 00:17:32.350
in a law enforcement
role should only be used

00:17:32.350 --> 00:17:34.190
as a matter of last resort,

00:17:34.190 --> 00:17:37.323
and only in the most urgent
and dire of situations.

00:17:38.200 --> 00:17:41.350
We are not in one of those situations now.

00:17:41.350 --> 00:17:42.470
- So it's not clear how long

00:17:42.470 --> 00:17:44.910
